what does this project do?
This is Spendly, a Flask-based expense tracker web app — it looks like a step-by-step learning project (a course/tutorial scaffold, judging by comments like "Students will write this file in Step 1" and route stubs marked "coming in Step 3/4/7/8/9").

Current state:
- Working: landing page, register page, login page (routes just render templates so far)
- Stubbed/TODO: logout, profile, add/edit/delete expense routes — all placeholder text, no logic yet
- Database layer (database/db.py) is empty — meant to be built out with get_db(), init_db(), seed_db() using SQLite
- Uses Flask 3.1, Werkzeug, and pytest for testing

So functionally it's meant to become a full expense-tracking app (users register/login, then add/edit/delete their expenses), but right now it's just scaffolding with the auth pages and DB layer unimplemented.

 what tech stack does this project use
 Backend: Python / Flask 3.1 (with Werkzeug), using SQLite for the database (per the database/db.py stub).

Frontend: server-rendered Jinja2 templates (templates/*.html) with plain CSS (static/css/style.css) and vanilla JavaScript (static/js/main.js) — no frontend framework.

Testing: pytest + pytest-flask.

No ORM, no build tooling (webpack/vite etc.) — it's a minimal, classic Flask stack.

Explain the project sttucture to me
Here's the layout of Spendly:

expense-tracker/
├── app.py                  Flask entry point — all routes defined here
├── requirements.txt        Python deps (flask, werkzeug, pytest, pytest-flask)
├── .gitignore              excludes venv/, the sqlite db, caches, .env
│
├── database/
│   ├── __init__.py         empty — makes `database` an importable package
│   └── db.py               empty stub — meant to hold get_db()/init_db()/seed_db() (SQLite)
│
├── templates/               Jinja2 HTML templates rendered by Flask
│   ├── base.html            shared layout: nav bar, footer, links to CSS/JS — other pages extend this via {% block %}
│   ├── landing.html         home/marketing page (route: "/")
│   ├── login.html           login page (route: "/login")
│   └── register.html        signup page (route: "/register")
│
└── static/                  assets served directly, referenced via url_for('static', ...)
    ├── css/style.css        site styling
    └── js/main.js           client-side JS

How it fits together:
- app.py is the single Flask app — no blueprints, no app factory, just one file with @app.route handlers.
- Each route renders a template from templates/, which extends base.html for shared nav/footer.
- static/ holds CSS/JS, wired into base.html via url_for.
- database/db.py is currently empty — the intended data layer for expenses/users, not yet built.
- Several routes (logout, profile, expenses/add|edit|delete) exist only as placeholder strings — no real logic, no DB writes yet.

So it's a very flat, no-framework-magic structure: one app file, templates, static assets, and a not-yet-implemented database module — consistent with it being an incremental teaching project.